<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  The simple physics: the one set of rules every simple airplane flies by, for JSBSim v1.3.3. FSG's own
  file: new in FSG, milestone 3 re-planned (docs/milestones/3-replan.md, pull request 3). An airplane's own
  file (aircraft/f4c-simple/f4c-simple.xml is the first) loads it with
      <aerodynamics file="../simple/aero.xml"/>
  and declares the numbers below. Nothing here belongs to one airplane.

  What it is. Lift, drag and three turning moments as plain forces, worked out from thirty-four
  numbers of the kind a spec sheet has (stall speeds, trim speed, g limit, roll rate) and a few set by
  feel. It stalls, it bleeds speed in a hard turn, and it comes back to its trim speed. Nothing is scripted.

  Where the rules came from. Wings (135crewdog/wings, src/sim/aero.js and src/sim/card.js, commit 6d689b7),
  whose F-4C the owner flew and liked. Its code is not copied: each rule is written again as a JSBSim
  function, and where one was made simpler on the way, the rule below says so.
    Lift       Wings' lift curve: straight up to the most lift, at the stall angle, then a drop over a few
               degrees onto a flat plate's lift (a mush). The most lift comes from the stall speed: at the
               stall, lift equals weight (card.js, CLmax). Flaps lift the whole curve by what the landing
               stall speed says, and the stall angle stays where it was (aero.js, liftCurve). Simpler than
               Wings: one wing, not two halves, so no wing drops by itself and nothing autorotates; and
               stalled air attaches again the moment the angle comes back.
    Drag       The airplane's own drag, more of it through Mach 1 (Wings' drag rise, Mach 0.85 to 1.05),
               plus the drag that comes with lift, as lift squared, plus the gear's, the flaps' and the
               speedbrakes'. Stalled: a flat plate's drag. Simpler than Wings: above the drag rise the two
               drag numbers are flat, where Wings has a line through two top speeds and a lift slope that
               changes with Mach; and there is no ground effect.
    Pitch      The nose comes back to the angle of attack that holds the trim speed (Wings: Cm0 and Cma,
               "Stick neutral, the airplane balances at the lift it needs at the neutral speed"), with
               Wings' two kinds of damping. The tail's push is in proportion to the stabilator's angle.
               How stiff the nose is follows from the tail: full back stick, when slow, holds a little past
               the stall angle (Wings: 1.2 times). Flaps lift without pitching (Wings). Simpler than Wings:
               no extra bite on the runway (card.js, rotateBite): one tail power lifts the nosewheel and
               stops the pull in the air, and the nose is about twice as stiff as Wings' for it.
    Roll, yaw  Wings' plain numbers for the wing's and the fin's effects (card.js: CYb, Clb, Cnb, Cnr), the
               roll damping its two-half wing gave (about -0.29), and aileron power sized so that full
               stick gives the roll rate at its speed. A stalled wing loses most of its ailerons' power
               (new here: one number; Wings' two halves stalled one at a time and could spin).
  The control run (aircraft/simple/controls.xml) puts the surfaces where the tilt asks, through one curve
  against speed. This file reads the surfaces, as the air does.

  THE NUMBERS AN AIRPLANE'S FILE DECLARES, each as <property value="..."> simple/... </property>:
    Lift
      simple/stall-clean-kt        stall speed clean, knots (calibrated, 1 g, sea level)
      simple/stall-landing-kt      stall speed with full flaps, knots
      simple/lift-slope            lift for each radian of angle of attack: with the stall speed it sets the stall angle
      simple/stall-width-deg       how many degrees past the stall angle the lift takes to fall away: small is a sharp stall
      simple/mush-lift             the most lift a fully stalled wing still makes (a flat plate: about 0.85)
    Drag
      simple/drag-clean            the airplane's own drag, below Mach 0.85: with the thrust it sets the top speed low down
      simple/drag-supersonic       the same above Mach 1.05: it sets the top speed up high
      simple/drag-in-a-pull        the drag that comes with lift, below Mach 0.85: how much speed a hard turn costs
      simple/drag-in-a-pull-supersonic   the same above Mach 1.05
      simple/drag-gear, simple/drag-flaps, simple/drag-boards   what the gear, full flaps and open speedbrakes add
      simple/boards-pitch          how the open speedbrakes pitch the nose (negative: down)
    Pitch
      simple/trim-speed-kt         the speed it holds with the stick at neutral, clean, knots
      simple/g-limit               the g that full back stick asks for above corner speed
      simple/g-limit-push          the negative g that full forward stick asks for when fast
      simple/full-back-over-stall  full back stick, slow, holds this many times the stall angle (Wings: 1.2)
      simple/tail-power            how hard the stabilator pushes at full travel nose up: with the wheels' place it sets the rotation speed
      simple/elevator-up-deg       the stabilator's travel, trailing edge up (stick back), degrees
      simple/elevator-down-deg     its travel the other way, degrees: forward stick has this share of the back stick's power
      simple/pitch-damping         how much the air resists the nose pitching (negative)
      simple/pitch-damping-alpha   how much it resists the angle of attack changing: settles the nose, costs no g (negative)
    Roll
      simple/roll-rate-dps         the roll rate full stick gives, degrees a second, at ...
      simple/roll-rate-at-kt       ... this speed, knots. Faster, the ailerons move less and the rate holds
      simple/aileron-deg           each aileron's travel, degrees
      simple/roll-damping          how much the wing resists rolling (negative)
      simple/stalled-aileron       the share of the ailerons' power left on a stalled wing, 0 to 1
      simple/dihedral              how a sideslip rolls it (negative: away from the slip, the stable way)
    Yaw
      simple/side-force            the sideways push of a sideslip (negative)
      simple/yaw-stiffness         how hard the fin swings the nose into a sideslip
      simple/yaw-damping           how much the air resists the nose swinging (negative)
      simple/rudder-power          the swing full rudder gives
      simple/rudder-deg            the rudder's travel each way, degrees
      simple/adverse-yaw           how much full aileron swings the nose the wrong way
  The airplane's own file also gives JSBSim its wing area, span and chord (metrics), its weight and
  inertias, its wheels and its engines.

  What this file gives back, for the control run, the tests and the game's settings:
      simple/stall-angle-rad   the stall angle (the tests' "stalled" is an angle of attack beyond it)
      simple/trim-angle-rad    the angle of attack that holds the trim speed
      simple/nose-stiffness    how hard the nose is pulled back to that angle (negative)
      simple/stalled           0 flying, 1 fully stalled
      simple/corner-speed-kt   the stall speed times the square root of the g limit

  Units and signs are JSBSim's: angles in radians unless a name says degrees, speeds in ft/s, forces in lb.
  A surface's angle is positive trailing edge down (the rudder: left), so a stabilator angle that pulls
  the nose up is negative.
-->
<aerodynamics>

 <!-- ==== the air ==== -->

 <!-- Dynamic pressure, lb/sq ft, for each knot squared at sea level: half the sea-level density, 0.0023769 slug/cu ft, times 1.68781 squared. -->
 <function name="simple/q-per-kt2">
  <value> 0.0033855 </value>
 </function>
 <!-- The true airspeed, never less than 30 ft/s: the damping terms divide by it. -->
 <function name="simple/v-fps">
  <max> <property> velocities/vt-fps </property> <value> 30 </value> </max>
 </function>
 <!-- Half the span, and half the chord, over the speed: what turns a roll, yaw or pitch rate into a fraction. -->
 <function name="simple/b2v">
  <quotient> <property> metrics/bw-ft </property> <product> <value> 2 </value> <property> simple/v-fps </property> </product> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/c2v">
  <quotient> <property> metrics/cbarw-ft </property> <product> <value> 2 </value> <property> simple/v-fps </property> </product> </quotient>
 </function>
 <!-- Dynamic pressure times wing area, lb, never less than 1. -->
 <function name="simple/qs">
  <max> <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> </product> <value> 1 </value> </max>
 </function>

 <!-- ==== lift ==== -->

 <!-- The most lift, clean: at the clean stall speed, at sea level, lift equals weight. -->
 <function name="simple/lift-max">
  <quotient>
   <property> inertia/weight-lbs </property>
   <product> <property> simple/q-per-kt2 </property> <property> simple/stall-clean-kt </property> <property> simple/stall-clean-kt </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> </product>
  </quotient>
 </function>
 <!-- The most lift with full flaps, from the landing stall speed the same way; what the flaps add now is their share of the difference. -->
 <function name="simple/lift-max-landing">
  <quotient>
   <property> inertia/weight-lbs </property>
   <product> <property> simple/q-per-kt2 </property> <property> simple/stall-landing-kt </property> <property> simple/stall-landing-kt </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> </product>
  </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/lift-flaps">
  <product>
   <difference> <property> simple/lift-max-landing </property> <property> simple/lift-max </property> </difference>
   <property> fcs/flap-pos-norm </property>
  </product>
 </function>
 <!-- The stall angle: where the straight part of the lift curve reaches the most lift. Flaps do not move it. -->
 <function name="simple/stall-angle-rad">
  <quotient> <property> simple/lift-max </property> <property> simple/lift-slope </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <!-- The lift that holds level flight at the trim speed, and the angle of attack that makes it. -->
 <function name="simple/lift-trim">
  <quotient>
   <property> inertia/weight-lbs </property>
   <product> <property> simple/q-per-kt2 </property> <property> simple/trim-speed-kt </property> <property> simple/trim-speed-kt </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> </product>
  </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/trim-angle-rad">
  <quotient> <property> simple/lift-trim </property> <property> simple/lift-slope </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/corner-speed-kt">
  <product> <property> simple/stall-clean-kt </property> <pow> <property> simple/g-limit </property> <value> 0.5 </value> </pow> </product>
 </function>

 <!-- How stalled the wing is: 0 up to the stall angle, 1 from stall-width-deg beyond it, a smooth step between (Wings' smoothstep), nose up or down alike. -->
 <function name="simple/stall-x">
  <max>
   <value> 0 </value>
   <min>
    <value> 1 </value>
    <quotient>
     <difference> <abs> <property> aero/alpha-rad </property> </abs> <property> simple/stall-angle-rad </property> </difference>
     <product> <property> simple/stall-width-deg </property> <value> 0.0174533 </value> </product>
    </quotient>
   </min>
  </max>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/stalled">
  <product>
   <property> simple/stall-x </property> <property> simple/stall-x </property>
   <difference> <value> 3 </value> <product> <value> 2 </value> <property> simple/stall-x </property> </product> </difference>
  </product>
 </function>
 <!-- Flying: lift in proportion to the angle of attack, plus the flaps', and no more than the most lift (nose down: the clean most lift, upside down). -->
 <function name="simple/lift-flying">
  <max>
   <product> <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/lift-max </property> </product>
   <min>
    <sum> <property> simple/lift-max </property> <property> simple/lift-flaps </property> </sum>
    <sum> <product> <property> simple/lift-slope </property> <property> aero/alpha-rad </property> </product> <property> simple/lift-flaps </property> </sum>
   </min>
  </max>
 </function>
 <!-- Stalled: a flat plate's lift, most at 45 degrees, none at 90. -->
 <function name="simple/lift-stalled">
  <product> <property> simple/mush-lift </property> <sin> <product> <value> 2 </value> <property> aero/alpha-rad </property> </product> </sin> </product>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/lift">
  <sum>
   <product> <difference> <value> 1 </value> <property> simple/stalled </property> </difference> <property> simple/lift-flying </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/stalled </property> <property> simple/lift-stalled </property> </product>
  </sum>
 </function>

 <!-- ==== drag ==== -->

 <!-- The drag rise: 0 below Mach 0.85, 1 from Mach 1.05, a smooth step between (Wings' MACH_DD and MACH_WAVE). -->
 <function name="simple/mach-x">
  <max> <value> 0 </value> <min> <value> 1 </value> <quotient> <difference> <property> velocities/mach </property> <value> 0.85 </value> </difference> <value> 0.2 </value> </quotient> </min> </max>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/mach-rise">
  <product>
   <property> simple/mach-x </property> <property> simple/mach-x </property>
   <difference> <value> 3 </value> <product> <value> 2 </value> <property> simple/mach-x </property> </product> </difference>
  </product>
 </function>
 <!-- The airplane's own drag: clean, more through Mach 1, plus what hangs out in the air. -->
 <function name="simple/drag-own">
  <sum>
   <property> simple/drag-clean </property>
   <product> <property> simple/mach-rise </property> <difference> <property> simple/drag-supersonic </property> <property> simple/drag-clean </property> </difference> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/drag-gear </property> <property> gear/gear-pos-norm </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/drag-flaps </property> <property> fcs/flap-pos-norm </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/drag-boards </property> <property> fcs/speedbrake-pos-norm </property> </product>
  </sum>
 </function>
 <!-- The drag that comes with lift, for each unit of lift squared. -->
 <function name="simple/drag-per-lift2">
  <sum>
   <property> simple/drag-in-a-pull </property>
   <product> <property> simple/mach-rise </property> <difference> <property> simple/drag-in-a-pull-supersonic </property> <property> simple/drag-in-a-pull </property> </difference> </product>
  </sum>
 </function>
 <!-- All of it. Flying: lift squared. Stalled: a flat plate's drag, twice the mush lift times the sine of the angle squared (Wings). -->
 <function name="simple/drag">
  <sum>
   <property> simple/drag-own </property>
   <product> <difference> <value> 1 </value> <property> simple/stalled </property> </difference> <property> simple/drag-per-lift2 </property> <property> simple/lift-flying </property> <property> simple/lift-flying </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/stalled </property> <value> 2 </value> <property> simple/mush-lift </property> <sin> <property> aero/alpha-rad </property> </sin> <sin> <property> aero/alpha-rad </property> </sin> </product>
  </sum>
 </function>

 <!-- ==== pitch ==== -->

 <!-- The angle of attack full back stick holds when slow: a little past the stall. -->
 <function name="simple/full-back-angle-rad">
  <product> <property> simple/full-back-over-stall </property> <property> simple/stall-angle-rad </property> </product>
 </function>
 <!--
  How stiff the nose is: the pull back toward the trim angle for each unit of the sine of the angle of
  attack. It follows from the tail: with the stabilator at full travel the nose settles at the full-back
  angle, so the stiffness is the tail's power over the way from the trim angle to there.
 -->
 <function name="simple/nose-stiffness">
  <quotient>
   <product> <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/tail-power </property> </product>
   <difference> <sin> <property> simple/full-back-angle-rad </property> </sin> <sin> <property> simple/trim-angle-rad </property> </sin> </difference>
  </quotient>
 </function>
 <!-- The tail: its power at full travel nose up, in proportion to the stabilator's angle (negative is trailing edge up, nose up). -->
 <function name="simple/tail">
  <product>
   <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/tail-power </property>
   <quotient> <property> fcs/elevator-pos-deg </property> <property> simple/elevator-up-deg </property> </quotient>
  </product>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/pitch">
  <sum>
   <product> <property> simple/nose-stiffness </property> <difference> <sin> <property> aero/alpha-rad </property> </sin> <sin> <property> simple/trim-angle-rad </property> </sin> </difference> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/pitch-damping </property> <property> velocities/q-aero-rad_sec </property> <property> simple/c2v </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/pitch-damping-alpha </property> <property> aero/alphadot-rad_sec </property> <property> simple/c2v </property> </product>
   <property> simple/tail </property>
   <product> <property> simple/boards-pitch </property> <property> fcs/speedbrake-pos-norm </property> </product>
  </sum>
 </function>

 <!-- ==== roll and yaw ==== -->

 <!--
  The aileron's power at full travel: what balances the wing's roll damping at the roll rate, at its speed
  (a steady roll: the two cancel). Sea level, so the speed in ft/s is the knots times 1.6878.
 -->
 <function name="simple/aileron-power">
  <product>
   <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/roll-damping </property>
   <property> simple/roll-rate-dps </property> <value> 0.0174533 </value>
   <quotient> <property> metrics/bw-ft </property> <product> <value> 2 </value> <property> simple/roll-rate-at-kt </property> <value> 1.6878 </value> </product> </quotient>
  </product>
 </function>
 <!-- How far over the ailerons are, -1 to 1: the left one's angle over its travel (it goes down for a roll to the right). -->
 <function name="simple/aileron">
  <quotient> <property> fcs/left-aileron-pos-deg </property> <property> simple/aileron-deg </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <!-- The rudder the same way, positive nose right (JSBSim's angle is positive trailing edge left). -->
 <function name="simple/rudder">
  <quotient> <product> <value> -1 </value> <property> fcs/rudder-pos-deg </property> </product> <property> simple/rudder-deg </property> </quotient>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/roll">
  <sum>
   <product> <property> simple/dihedral </property> <property> aero/beta-rad </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/roll-damping </property> <property> velocities/p-aero-rad_sec </property> <property> simple/b2v </property> </product>
   <product>
    <property> simple/aileron </property> <property> simple/aileron-power </property>
    <difference> <value> 1 </value> <product> <property> simple/stalled </property> <difference> <value> 1 </value> <property> simple/stalled-aileron </property> </difference> </product> </difference>
   </product>
  </sum>
 </function>
 <function name="simple/yaw">
  <sum>
   <product> <property> simple/yaw-stiffness </property> <property> aero/beta-rad </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/yaw-damping </property> <property> velocities/r-aero-rad_sec </property> <property> simple/b2v </property> </product>
   <product> <property> simple/rudder-power </property> <property> simple/rudder </property> </product>
   <product> <value> -1 </value> <property> simple/adverse-yaw </property> <property> simple/aileron </property> </product>
  </sum>
 </function>

 <!-- ==== the forces and moments JSBSim adds up ==== -->

 <axis name="LIFT">
  <function name="aero/force/lift-lbs">
   <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> <property> simple/lift </property> </product>
  </function>
 </axis>
 <axis name="DRAG">
  <function name="aero/force/drag-lbs">
   <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> <property> simple/drag </property> </product>
  </function>
 </axis>
 <axis name="SIDE">
  <function name="aero/force/side-lbs">
   <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> <property> simple/side-force </property> <property> aero/beta-rad </property> </product>
  </function>
 </axis>
 <axis name="ROLL">
  <function name="aero/moment/roll-lbsft">
   <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> <property> metrics/bw-ft </property> <property> simple/roll </property> </product>
  </function>
 </axis>
 <axis name="PITCH">
  <function name="aero/moment/pitch-lbsft">
   <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> <property> metrics/cbarw-ft </property> <property> simple/pitch </property> </product>
  </function>
 </axis>
 <axis name="YAW">
  <function name="aero/moment/yaw-lbsft">
   <product> <property> aero/qbar-psf </property> <property> metrics/Sw-sqft </property> <property> metrics/bw-ft </property> <property> simple/yaw </property> </product>
  </function>
 </axis>

</aerodynamics>
